//! The `outline-style` [CSS](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS) property sets the
//! style of an element's outline. An outline is a line that is drawn around an element, outside the
//! `border`.
//!
//! Initial: none. Inherited: no.
//! Percentages: not accepted.
//!
//! Keyword effects:
//! - auto: Permits the user agent to render a custom outline style.
//! - dashed: The outline is a series of short line segments.
//! - dotted: The outline is a series of dots.
//! - double: The outline is two single lines. The `outline-width` is the sum of the two lines and
//! the space between them.
//! - groove: The outline looks as though it were carved into the page.
//! - inset: The outline makes the box look as though it were embedded in the page.
//! - none: No outline is used.
//! - outset: The opposite of `inset`: the outline makes the box look as though it were coming out of
//! the page.
//! - ridge: The opposite of `groove`: the outline looks as though it were extruded from the page.
//! - solid: The outline is a single line.
//!
//! Grammar: auto | <outline-line-style>
//!
//! Specification: https://drafts.csswg.org/css-ui-4/#propdef-outline-style
//!
//! Author guide: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/outline-style
//!
//! Purpose and value descriptions adapted from MDN contributors (CC-BY-SA-2.5).
//!
//! Generated from @webref/css 8.7.8. Do not edit.

